13/Jan/26 3:55 PM;632881baa84c7f79c3833b05;Root Cause analysis provided by Claude Desktop:

h2. Root Cause Analysis

The new UI repeatedly steals window focus because {{focusInitialElementWhenReady()}} is being called multiple times rather than just once during initial screen rendering.

*Location of the issue:*

The primary problem is in {{client-v2/projects/framework/dynamic-forms/src/dynamic-form.component.ts}} (lines 236-249). There is an Angular {{effect()}} that monitors when all dynamic form fields are ready and then calls focus:

typescript

{noformat}effect(() => {
  const dynamicFormFields = this.jmfDynamicFormFields();
  const controls = this.controls();

  const count = dynamicFormFields.filter(
    (field) => field.componentRef() !== null,
  ).length;
  if (count !== controls.length) {
    return;
  }

  this.focusTrap?.focusTrap?.focusInitialElementWhenReady();
});{noformat}

*Why this causes the problem:*

This effect tracks the {{componentRef()}} signal from each {{DynamicFormFieldDirective}}. Whenever any field's component reference changes (during re-renders, data updates, or validation changes), the effect re-runs and calls {{focusInitialElementWhenReady()}} again. On Windows, focusing an element in the application causes the window to come to the foreground, which is why JMC keeps stealing focus from other applications like CMD.

A similar pattern exists in {{client-v2/projects/framework/common/portals/lock-screen/src/lock-screen.component.ts}} (line 99) where the lock screen also calls {{focusInitialElementWhenReady()}} inside an effect.

*Why the old UI doesn't have this problem:*

