17/Mar/26 9:19 AM;712020:03eb0e30-3eea-4831-9533-05d046229d05;Our solution was to add the following indexes to {{sls_trans_summary}}:

{noformat}CREATE INDEX idx_trans_summary_device_bu ON trans_summary (business_unit_id, device_id);
CREATE INDEX idx_trans_summary_training_mode ON trans_summary (training_mode);{noformat}

In the meantime, feel free to go ahead and add these indexes directly to your database, that will resolve the issue right away without needing to wait for the 250.1.10 release.

19/Mar/26 8:19 AM;5c8fc1cd627d3b2d0163b7f1;A review of the promotion engine rules in relation to the usage of business_unit_group_id on the prm_assigned_promotion table.
To use the business unit group to control the promotion activation a record must exist in the ctx_business_unit_grp table that defines the group with a group_type = PROMOTE.
Business units are added to the group through the ctx_business_unit_grp_lnk table with the same group type.

With regards to prm_assigned_promotion columns business_unit_group_id and tag_business_unit_id, the promotion validation checks:
 A. If tag_business_unit_id is set (not "*"), the current store must match this specific value
 B. If business_unit_group_id is set, the current store must be a member of that group
 C. When BOTH are set, the store must satisfy BOTH conditions

For example:
Groups:

!groups.jpg|width=217,alt="groups.jpg"!

Scenarios:

!scenario.jpg|width=562,alt="scenario.jpg"!

One key reason for using business_unit_group_id is to reduce the number of records necessary in the prm_assigned_promotion table. Therefore, you typically wouldn't include the tag_business_unit_id when using business_unit_group_id

----

"Store-Level Sync via Symmetric Node: Symmetric node should always sync at the store level. Although the prm_assigned_promotion table will contain business_unit_group_id at the CO level, the synchronization will always occur at the store level using tag_business_unit_id."
{color:#4c9aff}*Response:*{color}

* {color:#0747a6}The new router we are providing uses a view to determine which business units receive promotion records based on the following:{color}
*# {color:#0747a6}If a business_unit_group_id is provided (not *), ALL business units linked to the group will receive the promotion records regardless of the tag_business_unit_id value.{color}
*# {color:#0747a6}If a business_unit_group_id is * AND a tag_business_unit_id is provided (not *), just that single business unit will receive the promotion records.{color}
*# {color:#0747a6}If BOTH business_unit_group_id AND tag_business_unit_id are * then ALL stores will receive the promotion records.{color}
{color:#0747a6}The router will not alter the data as it arrives at the business unit.  Specifically, if a business_unit_group_id is supplied and tag_business_unit_id is set to wildcard (*), the router will not generate specific tag_business_unit_id values.  The data will remain identical to how it exists in the central database.{color}


"Group-Level and Store-Level Support: We should support both Group-level and Store-level synchronization to ensure flexibility across different use cases."
{color:#4c9aff}*Response:*{color}

* {color:#0747a6}Please refer to the router explanation provided above.{color}


"Handling Cancellations via Wildcard Sync: CO data will be inserted at the group level and then synced to BOs for all 6 tables at the tag_business_unit_id level with group_id as a wildcard (*). This approach is especially helpful during cancellations. For example, if Group1 has 45 stores with an effective start date of 1st Jan 2026 and end date of 1st Jan 2027, and a cancellation comes in for 5 stores, a new Group2 will be created for those 5 stores with the same start date but an updated end date of 18th March 2026. Since all primary keys remain the same and only the end date differs, the sync will correctly update the existing records at the BO level for those 5 stores."
{color:#4c9aff}*Response:*{color}

* {color:#0747a6}"CO data will be inserted at the group level and then synced to BOs for all 6 tables at the tag_business_unit_id level with group_id as a wildcard (*)" - This approach is not supported by the current architecture, as explained in the router logic above. When you use a group, SymmetricDS will sync the data with that group designation, and the tag_business_unit_id will remain the value it was created with. The group will NOT be changed to *, nor will the tag_business_unit_id change from its original value.{color}
{color:#0747a6}To accomplish this scenario, you would update the existing promotion with a new end date of March 18, 2026, so ALL stores reflect the new end date.  Next, create a new promotion with a NEW group that excludes the 5 stores. This promotion can start prior to March 18, 2026 (or even on Jan 1, 2026) and run through Jan 1, 2027.  Then all but the 5 stores will continue to allow the promotion after the March date.{color}


"Primary Key Design is Critical: for single table for simple promotion When consolidating from six tables into one, the primary key design must be carefully thought through, as it will be the foundation for handling cancellations and ensuring accurate record updates effectively."
{color:#4c9aff}*Response:*{color}

* {color:#0747a6}This refers to the new process/table that Stephen is providing, which uses a single table that is synchronized and then expanded to the 6 primary tables (not consolidated from 6 to 1).  In this case, the primary key is defined on the single table and will not have any conflicts with the other tables.  The intent for this table is strictly to handle simple single item promotions so the primary key should be basic.  {color}
{color:#0747a6}If your question concerns using the existing 6 tables to create this single table, that is not the intended approach.  The integration should directly load the new table and the existing 6 tables should not be part of the load.{color}
* {color:#0747a6}Note: All non-simple single item promotions will continue to be loaded into the 6 main promotion tables and are not part of this single table approach.{color}

12/Mar/26 10:31 AM;6299f8c41a2bdf007096c71d;[~accountid:qm:d8936ec3-5736-46a1-a0a2-e185d8b412c9:996aa0a9-1d03-4d80-96b2-483addbeba32] I do not have access to your extension layer, but this should have been called out.

Please update any and all usage of “generateServiceId” with the new component “AdyenServiceIdRegistry”



It is already provided in the abstract endpoint as a protected field (if your override endpoint already extends that).

You would replace

{noformat}adyenDeviceManager.createServiceID(){noformat}

with

{noformat}serviceIdRegistry.createServiceId(request.getDeviceId()){noformat}





If your payment endpoints do not currently extend the abstract endpoint, then please wire in the component like this:

{noformat}@Autowired
protected AdyenServiceIdRegistry serviceIdRegistry;{noformat}

