**** LMG Example:*

Here are the steps to accomplish this.  You will want to review to make sure you understand what is happening here and that this is making assumptions on the data I have seen.  For the promotion ones it will try to match based on the 4th position of the field parsed on :   If the field has more or less than 5 elements in the id it will send to every node (store).  If equal to 5 then only matching the 4th position will send any other record that has 5 but not a valid 4th element it will drop that data.  So, be very careful to know exactly what your data will look like.  Similar to the qualification and reward checks, the below is making assumptions.

Create a router with the following for tables with promotion_id in them (prm_promotion, prm_qualification & prm_reward)

{noformat}for(node : nodes) {
    String installationId = node.getExternalId();

    if (PROMOTION_ID != null) {
        String[] parsedPromotionId = PROMOTION_ID.split(":");

        if (parsedPromotionId.length == 5) {
            if (parsedPromotionId[3].trim().equals(installationId.trim())) {
                targetNodes.add(node.getNodeId());
            }
        } else {
            targetNodes.add(node.getNodeId());
        }
    }
}{noformat}

!image-20250723-191939.png|width=1317,height=728,alt="image-20250723-191939.png"!

Clicking in the Router Expression will open a popup window where you enter the beanshell code:

!image-20250723-195203.png|width=1435,height=915,alt="image-20250723-195203.png"!

For the other tables you will follow a similar pattern.  Create a new router for each qual item and reward item.

Qualification (looks like you have 2 variations of the id, 1-has 6 positions where the 5th is the store and 2- 3 positions where the third one has store + some text):

{noformat}for(node : nodes) {
    String installationId = node.getExternalId();

    if (QUALIFICATION_ID != null) {
        String[] parsedQualificationId = QUALIFICATION_ID.split(":");

        if (parsedQualificationId.length == 6) {
            if (parsedQualificationId[4].trim().equals(installationId.trim())) {
                targetNodes.add(node.getNodeId());
            }
        } else if (parsedQualificationId.length == 3) {
            String thirdElement = parsedQualificationId[2].trim();
            if (thirdElement.length() >= 5 && thirdElement.substring(0, 5).equals(installationId.trim())) {
                targetNodes.add(node.getNodeId());
            }
        } else {
            targetNodes.add(node.getNodeId());
        }
    }
}{noformat}

Reward (only noticed examples with 6 positions in the reward id field):

{noformat}for(node : nodes) {
    String installationId = node.getExternalId();

    if (REWARD_ID != null) {
        String[] parsedRewardId = REWARD_ID.split(":");

        if (parsedRewardId.length == 6) {
            if (parsedRewardId[4].trim().equals(installationId.trim())) {
                targetNodes.add(node.getNodeId());
            }
        } else {
            targetNodes.add(node.getNodeId());
        }
    }
}{noformat}


Should look something like the following when done:

!image-20250723-194507.png|width=1445,height=571,alt="image-20250723-194507.png"!



Then once routers are validated and created, go to the table routing and find the corresponding prm_xxx tables and update the router they use from a corp to businessunit to the new ones you’ve added.  Select the trigger and click Edit then update the Router Id.
Similar to this:

!image-20250723-194655.png|width=1519,height=475,alt="image-20250723-194655.png"!



Next, test it out.  Create or edit a record in each of these tables and see if the outgoing batch reflects it getting routed properly either to a single store or all stores.

